<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Deliberately empty, and that is the decision this file records.

	     com.apple.security.app-sandbox is ABSENT. The bridge's whole job is to connect to a
	     unix socket under ~/Library/Application Support/Threading, which a sandboxed process
	     cannot reach without a container-relative grant that does not exist for that path — and
	     Threading itself is unsandboxed for the same class of reason (PTY operations). A
	     sandboxed bridge would fail to connect on every launch and be indistinguishable from an
	     app that was closed, which is precisely the state it exists to avoid.

	     The containment that does apply is the socket's own: the rendezvous lives in a 0700
	     directory, so only this user's processes can reach it at all, and the per-session token
	     in the path decides which session a message routes to. Neither is weakened by anything
	     here. Contrast Targets/ExtensionHelper, which runs code we did not write and is
	     sandboxed hard; the bridge runs only its own code and carries no capability an ordinary
	     process of this user does not already have.

	     Hardened runtime is on (ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME = YES) with no exceptions: the bridge
	     loads no plug-ins, allocates no JIT pages, and disables no library validation. -->
</dict>
</plist>
